# What must never enter the build context.
#
# There was no .dockerignore at all until the deployment question was asked, and its
# absence was load-bearing in the wrong direction: `COPY packages/ packages/` in
# deploy/Dockerfile landed the HOST's packages/*/dist, packages/*/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
# and packages/*/node_modules on top of what pnpm had just installed in the image.
#
# Three consequences, all bad, in order of how long they stay hidden:
#
#   1. tsc --build reads a tsbuildinfo written on the host, concludes every project is
#      up to date, and emits NOTHING. The image still works, because dist/ arrived in
#      the same COPY — so it ships artifacts compiled on a developer's machine, from
#      whatever their tree happened to contain, and the image's own build step is
#      decorative.
#   2. A clean build (CI, a fresh clone) has no host dist/ to inherit, so it is the
#      only place the real behaviour shows. The two differ, which is the worst
#      possible arrangement: the failure appears where nobody is watching.
#   3. node_modules copied from a host are pnpm symlinks into that host's store. They
#      resolved here by luck of matching paths; nothing makes that true in general.
#
# So the first three entries are correctness, not hygiene. The rest is bulk the image
# has no use for — .git alone is larger than everything the Dockerfile copies.
node_modules
**/node_modules
dist
**/dist
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

.git
.github
.gitignore
docs
scripts
.smtp-crosscheck-venv
coverage
**/coverage

# Secrets never belong in a context: a layer keeps them even if a later step deletes
# them, and deploy/.env is the one file an operator fills with real credentials.
.env
.env.*
deploy/.env
deploy/secrets
